WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Randy Meeks was born in late-1978 or early-mid&nbsp;
1979. He was the firstborn in his family;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.840
we never see his parents, but his mother&nbsp;
is mentioned in Scream 2. In 1982 or 1983,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.560
when he was 3 or 4, Randy got a younger&nbsp;
sister named Martha. The family lived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.040
the northern California town of Woodsboro,&nbsp;
where Randy went on to attend Woodsboro High&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.760
School with his friends Sidney Prescott,&nbsp;
Tatum Riley, Billy Loomis and Stu Macher.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.420
Randy was a self-professed geek and expert&nbsp;
of horror movies, particularly titles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.080
featured the original Scream Queen,&nbsp;
Jamie Lee Curtis. In September 1996,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.120
Randy was 17 or 18 years old and working&nbsp;
in a video store. He was fired. Twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.240
But they still brought him back for a&nbsp;
third stint. Guess his film knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.800
superseded what I can only assume is really&nbsp;
bad customer service or situational awareness.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.820
But holding a high school job would become the&nbsp;
least of Randy’s worries, when Woodsboro became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.080
the scene of a series of murders committed by a&nbsp;
killer in a five and dime store mask: Ghostface.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.560
To learn how Randy was an inspiration&nbsp;
for an entire subgenre of movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.460
how he almost played a very different role&nbsp;
in Scream 2, and how he left a legacy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.180
still marks the Scream franchise today,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.020
(metal music)

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.560
Welcome to Horror History. I&nbsp;
am Randy Meeks. Or at least I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.740
would be if I was a Scream character&nbsp;
instead of an internet personality.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.980
Portrayed by Jamie Kennedy, Randy was&nbsp;
a legacy character and a fan favorite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.940
While Casey Becker brought the audience into the&nbsp;
tension and fear of Scream in the opening scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.260
it was Randy Meeks who would take over the role&nbsp;
as the proxy of the movie audience. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.160
his controversial death is still hotly&nbsp;
debated two sequels after his last appearance.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.220
I’ll be analyzing his entire&nbsp;
influence on the Scream franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.080
so let's start at his 1996 introduction in the&nbsp;
small fictional town of Woodsboro, California.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.120
(mysterious music)

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.560
We first meet Randy outside Woodsboro High School on&nbsp;
a September Thursday morning, the day after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.580
the murder of Casey Becker, sitting with his&nbsp;
friend and crush Sidney, her boyfriend Billy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.300
her best friend Tatum and Tatum’s recent&nbsp;
boyfriend, Stu. Randy is the type of guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.020
to put sunglasses on just to catch this grape&nbsp;
in his mouth, then immediately take them off.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.220
He secretly laments his inability to date Sidney,&nbsp;
who has been with Billy for two years at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.940
point. It’s unclear when Randy and Sidney met,&nbsp;
but they’ve known each other long enough to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.960
natural and comfortable with each other. Sidney&nbsp;
doesn’t seem aware of Randy’s crush on her, or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.620
least doesn’t mention it, while Randy is always&nbsp;
just missing his “chance” to shoot his shot.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.340
– [RANDY] Do you think Sid will go out with me?
– [RANDY] There goes my chance with Sid...

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.220
– It’s too bad he never found out about&nbsp;
this compliment, intended for him.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.036
– [GHOSTFACE] Do you like Scary movies?&nbsp;

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:25.920
– [SIDNEY] I like that thing you’re doing&nbsp;
with your voice, Randy. It’s sexy.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.900
– [ZAC] He also has a friendly rivalry with Stu. They seem&nbsp;
to enjoy busting each other’s balls, which may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.340
stem from a rivalry over who is the true horror&nbsp;
expert of the group. Truth be told, all of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.270
know a thing or two about horror. Except Tatum,&nbsp;
who doesn’t know John Carpenter from Wes Craven.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.820
– [TATUM] You're starting to sound&nbsp;
like some Wes Carpenter flick.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.120
– Stu and Randy have also both&nbsp;
had longtime crushes on Sidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.100
which I imagine also helped fuel the rivalry.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.940
Our first impression of Randy is that he’s a&nbsp;
dork and a little bit of an outsider. He’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.600
only single in the group, which, according&nbsp;
to his rules, may be another factor in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.460
survival. He has no tact when trying to dunk&nbsp;
on Stu. And he can’t read the room, obliviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.300
describing gore and violence while his crush&nbsp;
with the murdered mother squirms in discomfort.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.900
Even his outfits are designed to&nbsp;
read “outsider”. Cynthia Bergstrom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.960
the costume designer on the original&nbsp;
Scream, intentionally designed Randy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.200
look with bright and bold colors to&nbsp;
say “look at me! I want to be seen!”

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.360
As Cynthia Bergstrom put it “Randy was obviously&nbsp;
intelligent but a bit goofy, and not really taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.260
that seriously. He had a big personality and&nbsp;
liked attention. Hence, the brighter colors.&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.380
Hush Puppies were very popular at&nbsp;
the time. They were huge and ok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.860
not so timeless. But totally&nbsp;
geeky and perfect for Randy.”

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.040
While attending Woodsboro High, Randy&nbsp;
worked at the one place a teenage horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.420
geek with little tact fits in. A video rental&nbsp;
store. Even there, he did not really fit in.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.940
His job not only gave him more&nbsp;
consistent access to movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.180
it also gave him a sense of authority&nbsp;
among his friends. When he and Stu are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.720
debating who could be the killer, Randy is&nbsp;
animated with a strong, confident voice.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.620
– [RANDY] See, the police are always off track with&nbsp;
this sh*t! If they'd watch Prom Night they’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:55.560
save time. There’s a FORMULA to it, a very&nbsp;
simple formula! Everybody’s a suspect!

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.720
– But when Sidney and Billy come up…

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.520
– [RANDY] …maybe Sidney won’t have sex with him.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.420
– It’s obvious both Billy and Stu intimidate&nbsp;
Randy and Randy is the first one to correctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.140
guess them as the killers. Initially it’s&nbsp;
just a guess based off of patterns he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.080
seen in movies, which is also why&nbsp;
he includes himself as a suspect.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.700
– [RANDY] You’re absolutely right. I'm the first to admit it. If&nbsp;
this were a scary movie, I’d be the prime suspect.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.380
– It’s not until the house party finale that Randy’s&nbsp;
approach gains credence. Up to this point, nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.400
considers the murders to actually be following&nbsp;
well-known horror movie patterns. Except Billy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.068
Stu, of course. So when Randy takes the stage to&nbsp;
present The Rules, it becomes his moment of power.

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:31.560
– [RANDY] There are certain&nbsp;
rules that one must abide by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.960
in order to successfully survive a horror movie…

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.440
– Like on the quad with his friends at the&nbsp;
beginning, and the video store with Stu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.751
Randy is in his element here, though&nbsp;
he’s unaware of how right he is.

00:04:41.845 --> 00:04:48.780
– [RANDY] Number one: You can never&nbsp;
have sex. Big no no. Sex equals death.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.020
– [SIDNEY] Hey Stu, didn’t you used to date Casey?
– [STU] Yeah for like two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:52.809
seconds.

00:04:52.809 --> 00:04:53.925
(Casey grunts)

00:04:53.925 --> 00:04:56.040
– Number two. Never drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.820
or do drugs. It’s the sin factor.
–  What movie is this from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.401
‘I Spit on Your Garage?’

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:02.801
(Tatum grunts)
(glass shatters)

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:09.170
– And number three. Never ever under any circumstances&nbsp;
say “I’ll be right back”. Cause you won’t be back.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.480
– Randy would soon break one of&nbsp;
his own rules, but before we unpack that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.840
we’ve got to take a break, so… I’ll be right back.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.000
– [crowd] OHH!!!!
– [Randy] You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.581
you push the laws… you end up dead. Alright,&nbsp;
I’ll see you in the kitchen with a knife.

00:05:23.581 --> 00:05:25.217
(impact)

00:05:28.984 --> 00:05:32.428
(creepy music)

00:05:32.428 --> 00:05:34.500
As I’ve discussed in my Scream&nbsp;
Things You Missed videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.100
Scream was built as a commentary on the&nbsp;
horror genre as a whole. Randy serves as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.920
the audience surrogate, he’s aware&nbsp;
of the typical horror movie tropes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.280
and he’s at least somewhat aware that he’s in&nbsp;
the midst of a horror film scenario himself.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.420
– [RANDY] It’s the millennium.&nbsp;
Motives are incidental.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.260
– Scream is a satire that leans hard on&nbsp;
its metacinema concept. It’s a movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.820
acknowledges other movies exist, while pretending&nbsp;
it’s not a movie at all. This is nothing new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.480
director Wes Craven’s own “New Nightmare”&nbsp;
did something similar two years earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.900
with Robert Englund playing Robert&nbsp;
Englund becoming Freddie Krueger.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.200
What sets Scream apart from other&nbsp;
slasher films and other meta-horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.920
movies is Randy. Randy’s acknowledgment of&nbsp;
the patterns and tropes of horror movies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.340
so spot on because the killers are ALSO&nbsp;
aware of the rules and following them.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.180
But what makes Randy the perfect audience&nbsp;
proxy isn’t just that he asks the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.960
questions we do while watching safely&nbsp;
at home; it’s that he can also be wrong.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.620
After “The Rules” speech, Randy takes a&nbsp;
call revealing that Principal Himbry has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.000
been ghosted… not like the ****ty millennial&nbsp;
version of ghosted, but gutted and strung up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.820
on the football field. Everyone leaves&nbsp;
to go catch a glimpse of the corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:37.200
leaving Randy drinking alone in the house,&nbsp;
apparently ignoring Rule #2. Ghostface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.740
sneaks up behind him, but gets distracted&nbsp;
upon hearing a screaming Sidney outside.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.763
(screams)

00:06:41.763 --> 00:06:43.267
– [SID] Help me! Help!

00:06:43.267 --> 00:06:45.180
– So that’s where they got the title of this movie from...

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.780
Randy eventually realizes danger is afoot,&nbsp;
and steps outside where he is awarded with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.080
a few smacks in the face from a terrified&nbsp;
Gale Weathers’s car phone. I’m assuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.140
he twisted his ankle or pulled a muscle&nbsp;
in his leg or something as he went down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.920
because all he can do is scoot himself away&nbsp;
to avoid getting hit by the fleeing news van,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.760
and when we next see him, he’s limping and&nbsp;
holding his leg. We know that it wasn’t one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.880
of the Ghostface killers who injured his&nbsp;
leg, because Stu is in the house fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.880
Dewey at that time, and Billy should still&nbsp;
be playing dead in the upstairs bedroom.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.840
The movie doesn’t show us where exactly&nbsp;
Randy was in between this and this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.220
but before anybody asks, NO. It was not Roman&nbsp;
who attacked his leg, that makes no sense.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.220
Anyway, Randy limps up to&nbsp;
Sid, who… pulls a gun on him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:24.240
unsure who to believe is the killer between&nbsp;
him and Stu. She locks both of them out. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.780
don’t see what happens behind the closed door&nbsp;
but we can hear Randy’s pleas to be let inside.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.880
– [Randy] Help me, he’s gone crazy!

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.660
– But it seems like he’s able to separate himself&nbsp;
from Stu just in time to be let into the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.700
where he repeats his theory to Sidney and Billy.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:40.920
– [RANDY] Please help me, Stu’s flipped out he’s gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.934
mad!
– We all go a little mad sometimes.

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:46.740
– [ZAC] He shoots Randy, hitting him in the&nbsp;
shoulder and sending him flying through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.920
the coffee table. This keeps him out of&nbsp;
the climax, but we can assume he was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.160
to hear Billy and Stu’s entire plot as&nbsp;
he continued to lie on the foyer floor.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.980
He comes back to shortly after Sidney defeats Stu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.280
but before he can finish telling her “I never&nbsp;
thought I’d be so happy to be a virgin…”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.660
he’s knocked right back out by Billy, where he&nbsp;
stays until both villains are taken care of.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:04.560
– [RANDY] Careful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.700
This is the moment when the supposedly dead&nbsp;
killer comes back to life.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.900
The argument could be made, within The Rules,&nbsp;
that Randy being a virgin superseded his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.980
drinking sin thus allowing him to survive. But&nbsp;
I personally think that the rules are more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.760
“best practices” and less of an “automatic&nbsp;
disqualification”. If you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.200
all of the survivors do break a rule at one point&nbsp;
or another. Sidney obviously loses her v-card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.660
Dewey says “he’ll be right back” at the precinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:31.140
Gale says she’ll be right back before heading&nbsp;
off with Dewey to investigate the abandoned car.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:36.000
As the sun rises on a September morning, Billy&nbsp;
Loomis and Stu Macher, the Ghostface killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.860
are dead (presumably). And our survivors, Sidney&nbsp;
Prescott, Gale Weathers, Dewey Riley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.680
and Randy Meeks leave all the&nbsp;
tropes and rules behind to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.087
quiet lives of contemplation. The&nbsp;
end. Not really, there’s a sequel.

00:08:48.087 --> 00:08:52.440
– Because, let's face it baby,&nbsp;
these days, you gotta have a sequel!

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.980
But before that sequel would be&nbsp;
released, Randy breaks another rule.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:57.480
– Sex equals death.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.540
– Perhaps due to his confidence that&nbsp;
threat was over, sometime between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.080
the Woodsboro murders and the start of&nbsp;
college, Randy Meeks breaks his number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.980
one rule (and probably the rules of the&nbsp;
video store). He gives his virginity to:

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.402
– [DEWEY] Karen Kolchak?

00:09:09.402 --> 00:09:10.493
– Karen Kolchak?

00:09:10.493 --> 00:09:11.700
– Yes. Karen Kolchak.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.080
– This is never shown directly, thankfully, and&nbsp;
described sparingly by Randy so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.120
how it all went down is a matter of imagination.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.540
Like maybe… it’s a late night at&nbsp;
the video store. Randy and Karen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.360
are stocking videos in the adult&nbsp;
section, like any normal shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.600
when their hands brush against each&nbsp;
other and things escalate from there.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.720
Despite his moment of weakness with&nbsp;
Karen, Randy still seems to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.500
some feelings for Sidney Prescott. I say&nbsp;
this, because he follows her to college.

00:09:35.091 --> 00:09:37.606
(impact)

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.654
(mysterious music)

00:09:43.654 --> 00:09:48.060
It's Spring 1997 when we catch up to Randy,&nbsp;
having graduated from Woodsboro High,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:51.840
and now enrolled in his freshman year at&nbsp;
Windsor College in Ohio. A quiet change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.780
from the blood-splattered landlines of&nbsp;
Woodsboro where this high school geek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.080
tries to come into his own, and leave&nbsp;
his traumatic past in the end credits.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.160
The evening of April 12th, fellow Windsor College&nbsp;
students Maureen Evans and Phil Stevens are both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.226
brutally killed at an early screening of the&nbsp;
in-movie franchise, Stab. Guess how they died.

00:10:06.226 --> 00:10:06.240
[CLIP]
[CLIP]

00:10:06.567 --> 00:10:07.792
(knife stab and grunt)

00:10:07.792 --> 00:10:09.173
(scream and knife stab)

00:10:09.173 --> 00:10:13.380
The next morning, April 13th, at the Windsor&nbsp;
College Film Theory class, we’re introduced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.220
a slightly new Randy Meeks. The bright colors&nbsp;
used for Randy’s high school wardrobe are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:22.140
absent and Randy sits calmly in a preppy maroon&nbsp;
polo shirt. The class debates reality versus film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:25.680
and this Randy doesn’t hesitate to&nbsp;
tell everyman creeper Mickey what’s up.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:28.740
– [MICKEY] It’s a classic case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.580
of life imitating art imitating life.
– [RANDY] Let me tell you about reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:37.380
Mickey. I lived through this. Okay? Life&nbsp;
is life. It doesn’t imitate anything.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.280
It’s a far cry from Randy’s relationship with&nbsp;
Billy which involved much more flinching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.240
shrinking, and apologies. Similarly, where&nbsp;
in the video store Randy argued and got loud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.440
to prove his point, college Randy has developed&nbsp;
some more confidence. Instead of being jeered by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:54.824
peers at a high school party, Randy takes Joshua&nbsp;
Jackson, aka Film Class Guy #1, to horror school.

00:10:54.824 --> 00:10:58.373
– [JOSHUA] Aliens is a classic&nbsp;
okay? “Get away from her, you b*tch!”&nbsp;

00:10:58.373 --> 00:11:01.980
– [RANDY] I believe the line is “Stay away from&nbsp;
her you b*tch.” This is film class, right?

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.400
– And… he’s wrong! But confidently&nbsp;
wrong. After an incredibly stressful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.320
high school career full of&nbsp;
toxic friends and murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.640
Randy has come into his own in college. This&nbsp;
is the beginning of a new act for Randy Meeks.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.500
–  [GIRL] So Mr. originality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:17.280
how would you make it different?
– [RANDY] I’d let the geek, get the girl.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.900
– Unfortunately, this would&nbsp;
also be Randy’s final act.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.500
– [SIDNEY] It’s starting again, Randy.
– [RANDY] It’s not. A lot of sh*t happens at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:30.540
movies. People get robbed, shot, maimed, murdered…&nbsp;
multiplex is a very dangerous place to be.”

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.917
– And that Sidney has a boyfriend. Again.

00:11:32.917 --> 00:11:34.320
– [RANDY] Hello Derek. How you doing?

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.420
– [Zac] Pre-med too. Whatever, he dies anyway.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:40.020
It’s interesting that Randy would choose to go&nbsp;
to an Ohio-based school for film in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.840
place when some of the most popular&nbsp;
schools for film studies are located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.920
in his home state of California. I assume&nbsp;
this was for one or two reasons. Perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:50.280
after the nightmare of his senior year at&nbsp;
Woodsboro, Randy opted for an environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:54.000
with as much distance from his teenage home&nbsp;
and least chance of murder as manageable.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:59.280
However, I believe Randy most likely chose Windsor&nbsp;
in Ohio to stay close to his crush and final girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.820
Sidney Prescott who was attending&nbsp;
Windsor for theater studies. Unwittingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:06.120
this choice put Randy right back to&nbsp;
the wrong end of a Ghostface blade.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:10.620
Any good friend would have encouraged Randy to&nbsp;
give up this longstanding crush on Sid. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:13.680
if you survive two slashers together&nbsp;
and the girl still isn’t into you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.820
where else is there to go? What can a gift of&nbsp;
flowers do that taking a bullet couldn’t? But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.360
then again, most of his friends are dead or&nbsp;
full-grown adults living in another state.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.440
In general, making friends as an adult can&nbsp;
be tricky. So he and Sidney are still close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.360
perhaps trauma-bonded in perpetuity.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:31.500
Regardless, Randy is initially reluctant to see&nbsp;
the mounting evidence even when presented by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.880
Sidney. But when news reporter Gale Weathers&nbsp;
arrives, Randy switches gears. Because Randy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.020
knows, once the legacy characters arrive, there’s&nbsp;
no stopping the horror movie plot from unfolding.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.867
– [RANDY] Sydney, look, it’s Gale Weathers!&nbsp;

00:12:41.867 --> 00:12:43.456
–  What?
–  Star of the Gale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.457 --> 00:12:47.280
Weathers press conference. Based on the book&nbsp;
by Gale Weathers. Soon to be a major motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.620
picture starring Gale Weathers.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:54.600
– Randy uses his trademark humor to mask what has&nbsp;
to be a moment of abject dread. Gale and Sidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.460
are intimately connected to Ghostface through the&nbsp;
September 1995 murder of Maureen Prescott. Judging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:04.080
by the events of Scream in 1996, when both are in&nbsp;
the same place, everyone becomes collateral.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.880
Then Deputy Dewey arrives.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:07.020
– [DEREK] Who was that guy?&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:07.920
– [SID] An old friend.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.740
– [RANDY] Deputy Dewey. Woodsboro’s&nbsp;
finest. What’s he doing here?

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:14.820
– Randy’s expression in this scene, more muted&nbsp;
and tight than while holding his own in class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:19.560
shows he’s aware of exactly what Deputy Dewey&nbsp;
is doing here. He also already knows, with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.460
horror expertise, where this plot goes when all&nbsp;
the survivors of the original film are all in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.080
place. This would have been a great time for Randy&nbsp;
to skip town. Isn’t it Spring Break or something?

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:32.940
But this is Randy. Randy, who came back to&nbsp;
Stu’s house for Sid after he knew Stu was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:37.560
the killer. This is Randy who stayed OUTSIDE&nbsp;
THE DOOR with Stu, the KILLER, when Sid locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.400
them out. This is Randy who wouldn’t leave&nbsp;
Sidney to die, even after getting shot.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:44.880
The reason the media is swarming Windsor&nbsp;
campus is that two students lost their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:48.180
lives in a screening of Stab. Rumors&nbsp;
that a series of serial killings could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:52.234
be happening again are spreading around the quad,&nbsp;
so naturally, there’s only one thing to do. Party.

00:13:52.518 --> 00:13:54.480
But first, despite his outward hesitance,

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:58.980
to fully accept Ghostface’s return, Randy&nbsp;
stops at home to record a very special video.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:00.720
– Told you I’d make a movie someday.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.780
– [ZAC] This video wouldn’t be seen by&nbsp;
audiences, or the Scream gang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:08.400
until the 2000 release of Scream 3. But there are&nbsp;
some details in Randy’s final recording that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:12.600
relevant to him in the Scream 2 portion of his&nbsp;
timeline. Let’s go to the tape. From the footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:16.500
we can determine a couple of things. Randy most&nbsp;
likely lives in a dorm and has a roommate named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:20.580
Paul who doesn’t knock when entering. And, since&nbsp;
Randy is sporting the same maroon polo shirt he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:24.060
wore in Film Theory class, we can assume he&nbsp;
recorded the video sometime after the press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:28.020
conference but before the sorority party where&nbsp;
he’s wearing a collared black button-up sweater.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:32.040
The tape reveals his encounter with Karen Kolchak&nbsp;
and provides the rules for a theoretical third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:36.180
rampage, just in case Randy doesn’t survive&nbsp;
this second one. We’ll come back to that.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:40.560
That night Randy goes to a greek house party.&nbsp;
Could anyone have imagined back in Woodsboro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:43.920
Randy Meeks walking into a gathering of&nbsp;
frat bros and sorority sisters out of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:48.540
own free will? In a black polo shirt, no less?&nbsp;
Well, Sidney was there, so I guess it tracks.

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:50.580
– [RANDY] Cocktail?
– [SIDNEY] What took you so long?

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:54.120
– And here’s another moment exemplifying&nbsp;
Randy’s maturity. Sure, he has a drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:58.020
waiting for Sidney. But he doesn’t hover&nbsp;
around, and ends up walking off with a blonde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.170
decked out in a black lace long-sleeve... uhhhh wait, no he doesn't.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.620
But this positive emotional development&nbsp;
doesn’t last long. Cops fill the street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:07.080
in front of the sorority house down&nbsp;
the road and that’s the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:10.560
Delta Lambda Zeta sorority party. And&nbsp;
the beginning of the worst reunion.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.780
– [RANDY] Nothing like a funeral&nbsp;
to bring family together.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:17.100
– The Windsor body count rises with the confirmed&nbsp;
death of fellow film classmate Cici Cooper on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:21.660
April 13th. Death by Ghostface. It’s not&nbsp;
long after when Sidney herself is targeted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:26.520
making it impossible to deny that Woodsboro is&nbsp;
happening again in the midwest. However, Randy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.360
already let go of his denial, as demonstrated by&nbsp;
his recording of the tape earlier that afternoon.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:35.460
April 14th, the day after Cici’s unfortunate&nbsp;
swan-dive, Randy sits down with former deputy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:39.540
Dewey. As they walk through the dining hall, he&nbsp;
expresses his frustration with his Stab casting.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:44.820
– [RANDY] I don’t get it. They get Tori Spelling to&nbsp;
play Sid and they cast Joe Blow Nobody to play me.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:48.660
– Having his likeness cast with “the guy&nbsp;
who drove Jane Seymore’s stagecoach”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:52.920
had to hurt Randy as a cinephile AND Sidney’s&nbsp;
self-proclaimed “love slave”. I think Randy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:56.640
too mature to take it completely seriously, but&nbsp;
it doesn’t help prevent him from backsliding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.660
his Woodsboro past. Interestingly, Randy’s&nbsp;
wardrobe also begins to reflect his past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:04.020
with a return to the brighter greens&nbsp;
of his teenage years. I believe this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.900
is to illustrate Randy regressing&nbsp;
as the past catches up to him.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:10.800
– [RANDY] The way I see it, someone’s out&nbsp;
to make a sequel. Cash in on all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.680
murder movie hoopla. So it’s our job&nbsp;
to observe The Rules of the sequel.

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.040
– [ZAC] Randy runs through The Rules much quicker&nbsp;
and much more succinctly than in Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.820
where he was admittedly drunk, and rattling off&nbsp;
the rules in between empty bottles winged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:24.120
at his head. This illustrates that&nbsp;
though Woodsboro is coming for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:28.260
he’s pushing back against Woodsboro. He doesn’t&nbsp;
just hope, he knows he can still survive.

00:16:29.442 --> 00:16:30.722
– [RANDY] Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.722 --> 00:16:33.960
the body count is always bigger.
Number two, death scenes are always much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:39.480
more elaborate. More blood, more gore, carnage&nbsp;
candy. Your core audience just expects it.&nbsp;

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:43.200
And number three if you want your sequel&nbsp;
to become a franchise, never ever…&nbsp;

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.840
– How do we find the killer&nbsp;
Randy? That’s what I want to know.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.740
– Dewey cuts off Randy before the third&nbsp;
rule. However, a teaser trailer featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:52.860
an extended version of the scene which&nbsp;
revealed the third rule would have been…

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:56.940
– Never, ever, under any circumstances&nbsp;
assume the killer is dead.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:00.960
– This was initially a reference to Randy’s last&nbsp;
line in the first movie and a deliberate in-joke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:04.500
by the crew as a nod that it’s impossible&nbsp;
to ensure a successful horror franchise.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.320
Before moving on, let’s look at&nbsp;
the production of Scream again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:11.340
as there were some decisions in the development&nbsp;
and on set that had major lasting effects for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:14.220
the entire franchise. Writer Kevin&nbsp;
Williamson had already planned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:18.720
two sequels before Scream 1996 was optioned.&nbsp;
However, during the development of Scream 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.740
the original script was leaked online revealing&nbsp;
the identity of the Windsor College killers.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:27.000
In the theatrical release of Scream 2, Randy&nbsp;
points out Sidney’s roommate Hallie as a suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.260
despite Dewey’s assertion that serial killers&nbsp;
are typically, as he puts it, “white males”:

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:35.940
– [RANDY] That’s why it’s perfect, it’s sort&nbsp;
of against the rules but not really. Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:39.780
Voorhes was a terrific serial killer and&nbsp;
there’s always room for Candyman’s daughter.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:43.560
– Randy also points to Sidney’s current&nbsp;
boyfriend Derek as the “Billy Loomis”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:47.280
the obvious killer posing as a boyfriend… but&nbsp;
quickly walks it back, claiming that the killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.180
would want to “break some new ground” and would&nbsp;
have “half a brain”. This is obviously Randy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:55.860
resentment towards Derek for dating Sid. But, had&nbsp;
the original script been produced, Randy wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:00.780
have been wrong. The leaked script included four&nbsp;
Ghostface killers: Hallie, Derek, Cotton and Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:03.240
Loomis. The&nbsp;
leaked script also had a very different role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:06.840
for Randy. Instead of attending college with&nbsp;
Sidney, he was Gale Weathers’s new cameraman.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.080
There’s no way to know how the original&nbsp;
Scream 2 script would have been received.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.020
But while most fans agree that four&nbsp;
killers would have been overdoing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.020
the fandom can be split on whether the&nbsp;
choice to have Randy behind the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:19.380
was the better direction. Personally, I&nbsp;
wouldn’t want to have to trade in Joel.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:22.740
What’s interesting, is though Randy&nbsp;
dies in the same manner in both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:26.040
leaked and produced script, it was&nbsp;
in Williamson’s rewrite that Randy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:29.340
provided The Rules to Surviving a Horror&nbsp;
Sequel. Strange to think The Rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:32.460
which are now a franchise staple,&nbsp;
were almost completely left behind.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.880
It was by unforeseen circumstances,&nbsp;
not by the original design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:39.480
that Scream 2 launched a meta-commentary&nbsp;
subgenre, and it never would have worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.720
without a character like Randy Meeks&nbsp;
at the center. Unfortunately for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.860
and his fans, the death he dodged in&nbsp;
Woodsboro eventually caught up with him.

00:18:47.517 --> 00:18:48.568
(impact)

00:18:51.873 --> 00:18:54.781
(dark upbeat music)

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:00.300
Gale, Dewey, and Randy are together on the quad&nbsp;
when Gale’s phone rings. Assuming it to be spam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:02.220
Randy picks up Gale’s phone in frustration,

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:03.540
– GALE’S NOT HERE.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:08.040
– [ZAC] Only to have Ghostface on the line. While Gale and&nbsp;
Dewey split off together to assault strangers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:12.180
phones of catching the caller, Randy attempts to&nbsp;
keep Ghostface on the line as long as possible.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:15.360
– [RANDY] So uh… what’s your favorite scary movie?

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.040
– The exchange quickly gets personal.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:20.460
– [GHOSTFACE] Why are you even&nbsp;
here Randy? You’ll never be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.233
leading man.

00:19:22.233 --> 00:19:23.835
– [RANDY] F****CK YOUUUU.

00:19:23.835 --> 00:19:28.440
– And Randy makes a critical error, a far more&nbsp;
fatal one than sleeping with Karen Kolchak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:32.640
If he’d been thinking clearly, he might have&nbsp;
remembered his own expertise. He might have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:36.180
at least, walked over to a group of people&nbsp;
just to avoid being alone. He might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:39.420
played it safe with his commentary&nbsp;
on the original Ghostface killers.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:44.220
– [RANDY] And Billy Loomis.&nbsp;Billy Loomis, what the f*ck.&nbsp;Jesus.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:49.620
What a rat lookin, homo repressed mama's&nbsp;
boy. Why not set your goals higher, huh?

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:53.400
– He couldn’t have known that that “mama’s boy’s”&nbsp;
mother was the very person he was speaking to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:57.240
and that line did not put him in her&nbsp;
good graces. Or take him off her list.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:01.920
Had he been less emotional, he might have&nbsp;
considered he was holding GALE’S phone, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:07.800
Ghostface was targeting GALE, as he wandered&nbsp;
aimlessly over to GALE’S van, where this happens.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:08.820
(stinger)

00:20:10.418 --> 00:20:11.657
(door shuts)

00:20:11.657 --> 00:20:13.022
(Randy grunts)

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:19.500
Randy’s death was and remains controversial among&nbsp;
fans. After all, he was relatable to the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:23.640
their proxy, the one character saying what anyone&nbsp;
would be thinking watching the horror unfold.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:27.720
–  It shouldn’t have been&nbsp;
Randy. It should have been me.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:29.460
– [ZAC] Imagine a world where THAT happens.

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:34.560
There’s some irony in that, after Randy takes a&nbsp;
solid stance on sequels being inferior in class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:39.360
a Stab sequel was guaranteed after he became&nbsp;
one of the victims. On top of that, his actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:43.860
proxy would probably be the lowest billed and,&nbsp;
despite it being fiction, he STILL probably died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:48.420
and didn’t get the girl. Maybe he can be&nbsp;
thankful that he passed away before he saw it.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:52.620
But death wasn’t the end of Randy Meeks. Or&nbsp;
at least his bloodline. His younger sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.540
Martha Meeks, was still alive and well.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.400
– [MARTHA]  Don’t shoot, I’m only seventeen!

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.640
– [ZAC] And now she was 17, about the same&nbsp;
age her brother was during the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.480
Ghostface encounter. In this interaction,&nbsp;
Randy’s lasting effect on others is clear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.100
Randy was loved and still missed as Martha,&nbsp;
Sidney, Dewey, and Gale share a somber moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:12.060
before Martha hands over the VHS tape that&nbsp;
Randy recorded before his untimely death.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:17.040
So… the tape. Before continuing into Randy’s&nbsp;
legacy after death, we should talk about why this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:22.020
tape exists and how it almost didn’t. Again, you&nbsp;
can’t fully dissect Scream without considering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:26.340
real-life development and production. Screenwriter&nbsp;
Kevin Williamson saw a lot of success with Scream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:30.480
With so many projects on the slate, he didn’t have&nbsp;
time to write the third one, though he did provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:35.100
an outline. Bob and Harvey Weinstein hired Ehren&nbsp;
Kruger to replace him. Surprisingly, this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:39.960
not go down as Harvey’s most heinous crime.&nbsp;
Ehren’s not that bad. Though Kruger did follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:45.060
Williamson’s outline initially, the backlash after&nbsp;
the 1999 Columbine massacre caused producers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:49.680
request a script change with a greater focus on&nbsp;
satire and less focus on teen-on-teen violence.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:54.000
In short, before production began, Scream&nbsp;
3 was a mess and needed something familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:58.200
and beloved to anchor the series back&nbsp;
to its 1990s roots. Due to the negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:02.340
feedback from fans over Randy’s death in&nbsp;
Scream 2, the answer seemed obvious. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:05.540
production considered different ways to&nbsp;
possibly bring him back from the dead-

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:11.340
But it was agreed Randy surviving that&nbsp;
brutal van takedown was too unbelievable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:15.060
(I’m looking at you, Fast and Furious)&nbsp;
no matter the retrofitted circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:16.920
so the pre-recorded tape was written in.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:21.000
Speaking of the tape and the unbelievable,&nbsp;
why did it take Martha two years to hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:24.840
over a tape recorded specifically to help&nbsp;
her dead brother’s friends? Maybe Randy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:28.800
explicitly told her to give it over only if&nbsp;
Ghostface was active, but five people died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:33.267
before she brought it! She could have mailed&nbsp;
it? Called and said she had it? Teenagers.

00:22:33.267 --> 00:22:35.460
– [RANDY] If you’re watching this&nbsp;
tape, it means, as I feared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.421
I didn’t survive the killings here&nbsp;
at Windsor College. &nbsp;

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:41.400
– Randy warns that if they find themselves&nbsp;
dealing with an unexpected backstory and a&nbsp;

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:44.640
preponderance of exposition, then&nbsp;
this isn’t just another sequel. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:48.540
the concluding chapter of the rare horror&nbsp;
trilogy with a new set of amended Rules:

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.880
– One. You've got a killer&nbsp;
who’s gonna be superhuman.&nbsp;

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.940
Number two. Anyone, including&nbsp;
the main character, can die.&nbsp;

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:56.940
Number 3. The past will come&nbsp;
back to bite you in the ass.

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:58.080
Basically, in the third one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:01.440
you gotta cryogenically freeze his&nbsp;
head, decapitate him, or blow him up.

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:05.700
– [ZAC] Or put him in an industrial shredder. Not&nbsp;
every aspect of Randy’s Rules for Surviving a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:10.500
Horror Sequel came to pass in Scream 3, but 100%&nbsp;
accuracy isn’t really the point of Randy Meeks.

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:14.340
In a 2021 interview, Kevin Williamson&nbsp;
reflected on the reaction to Randy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:17.520
death in Scream 2. “I remember people&nbsp;
took [Randy's death] very hard because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:21.180
at the time, that was a beloved character,&nbsp;
which, to me, is what keeps the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:24.780
moving... If you kill someone you really&nbsp;
love, it makes it personal for people.” Which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:28.260
yeah, the audience proxy or surrogate&nbsp;
dying does feel pretty damn personal.

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:32.280
Randy was the heart of the series; his&nbsp;
“expert” analysis could have been obnoxious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:36.540
or written only as a greek (geek) chorus,&nbsp;
with his relationship to the other legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:40.140
characters limited to supporting their own&nbsp;
characterization. Reportedly, production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:44.100
filmed at least two hours of footage with Jamie&nbsp;
Kennedy for the tape, and it was condensed down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:48.480
to a quick three minutes. Who knows what sort of&nbsp;
Randy gems were left on the cutting room floor?

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:52.260
The fourth Scream was released in&nbsp;
2011, a decade after Scream 3. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:55.260
the newly introduced characters are mostly&nbsp;
disconnected from the legacy characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:58.980
Randy’s influence continues with new&nbsp;
characters designating themselves as “The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:03.208
Randy” over their “expert” horror knowledge.&nbsp;
And, of course, this lasting impression.

00:24:03.208 --> 00:24:06.420
– [CHARLIE] Cinema Club thanks you for coming&nbsp;
out for our third annual Stab-a-Thon.

00:24:06.533 --> 00:24:08.951
(cheers erupt)

00:24:08.951 --> 00:24:13.260
– Robbie Mercer, VP of the Woodsboro High&nbsp;
Cinema Club, wears a bright green shirt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:16.800
under a light blue button-up; it’s a straight&nbsp;
cosplay of Randy’s look in Scream and Scream 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:20.640
which was immortalized in-universe in the&nbsp;
Stab films. It’s an oddly fitting tribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.360
that Robbie isn’t dressed as Randy but&nbsp;
the fictional character of Randy played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:28.860
by an extra from “Dr. Quinn Medicine Woman”.&nbsp;
Randy at least would get a kick out of it.

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:33.480
The fifth film, Scream (2022), would feature&nbsp;
Randy’s niece and nephew, the twin children of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.480 --> 00:24:37.860
sister Martha, Mindy and Chad Meeks. Mindy takes&nbsp;
up the mantle of “Randy” in the Scream requel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.000
evidence that his character is still just as&nbsp;
loved and missed in-movie as he is by fans.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.720
Kevin Williamson and Jamie Kennedy&nbsp;
have been clear that Randy is DEAD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:49.200
and not coming back. But his influence&nbsp;
is immortal. Though Randy died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:53.460
a concept inspired by him became its own trope in&nbsp;
the series. With the establishment of The Rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:57.960
throughout the original trilogy, Randy evolved&nbsp;
from “horror geek” to “horror expert”. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:01.740
his death, another character takes up this&nbsp;
responsibility, in the subsequent sequels.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:07.140
Should Randy have survived long enough to at least&nbsp;
anchor a full trilogy? Maybe. But the heartbreak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:10.980
of his loss gave weight to a satirical horror&nbsp;
series that was making it up as it went along.

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:15.720
Scream is a movie that acknowledges reality&nbsp;
while existing within its own. Randy isn’t “us”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:19.680
the audience. He’s who the audience feels&nbsp;
they could be. But would we, the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:23.580
were in the movie… are we sure we’d&nbsp;
survive to even the first sequel?

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:25.140
(cell phone rings)

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.840
Because the franchise rule now seems to dictate…

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:29.201
(cell phone rings)

00:25:29.201 --> 00:25:31.680
… that the Expert becomes the target.

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:33.150
(cell phone rings)

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:35.618
– [GHOSTFACE] What’s your favorite scary movie?

00:25:35.618 --> 00:25:39.360
– This could take awhile.&nbsp;
For more details on the Scream universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:41.596
check out these videos on Things You Missed. 

00:25:41.596 --> 00:25:43.740
 
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:49.200
horrors every week, ring the deathbell for all&nbsp;
notifications and I’ll see you in the next one.

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:54.240
– [RANDY] So in closing, let me say, Good luck,&nbsp;
Godspeed, and for some of you… I’ll see you soon.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:56.580
– [ZAC] Assuming we both survive.

